        "math_academy_id": "q-183672",
        "problem": "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB={{b}_{n}\\,:\\,{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+3{b}_{n}\\,,\\,{b}_{1}=1,\\,{b}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $B?$",
        "worked_solution": "The elements of $B$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${b}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n+2}={b}_{n+1}+3{b}_{n},\\,{b}_{1}=1,\\,{b}_{2}=4,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4 \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={b}_{2}+3{b}_{1} \\\\  & =4+3(1) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={b}_{3}+3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =7+3(4) \\\\  & =19 \\\\ {b}_{5} & ={b}_{4}+3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =19+3(7) \\\\  & =40 \\\\ {b}_{6} & ={b}_{5}+3{b}_{4} \\\\  & =40+3(19) \\\\  & =97 \\\\  & \u22ee\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB={1,4,7,19,40,97,\u2026}.\n$$",
